## Phase 2 - Prep (commands run by the USER; the agent NEVER runs sudo)

- [ ] Fully update the current release first:
  ```bash
  sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade
  ```
- [ ] Update any DKMS module whose pin excludes the target kernel (from the DKMS check
  above) to a target-kernel-capable release, then `sudo dkms autoinstall`. Secure Boot: a
  rebuilt module must be re-signed; enroll the MOK if prompted. See references/hazard-checks.md.
{% set ns = namespace(has_ppa=false) %}
{% for e in codename_entries %}
{% if "ppa" in e.uri or "launchpad" in e.uri %}
{% set ns.has_ppa = true %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if ns.has_ppa %}
- [ ] Revert conflicting PPAs to distro versions (avoids held-back upgrades), one per PPA:
{% for e in codename_entries %}
{% if "ppa" in e.uri or "launchpad" in e.uri %}
  - from `{{ e.uri }}` -> `sudo ppa-purge ppa:<owner>/<name>`
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
  Do any backports/staging PPA FIRST - a PPA shipping newer versions of distro packages is
  the most common upgrade blocker.
  `ppa-purge ppa:<owner>/<name>` defaults to the CURRENT release. Do NOT pass `-d` or a
  suite that is not in your sources yet. Read the owner/name from the URI path.
{% endif %}
- [ ] Protect the tools you identified above before any autoremove:
  `sudo apt-mark manual <packages>`. Then review the autoremove set; do NOT blindly purge.

## Phase 3 - Upgrade

- [ ] Run the upgrade (path must be open; NEVER use `-d` for a stable target):
  ```bash
  sudo do-release-upgrade
  ```
- [ ] Handle each conffile prompt deliberately. For each one, the agent classifies it with
  the `classify-conffile` helper (see references/conffile-classification.md for the
  invocation and the decision table). Default to KEEPING your version (N) unless you never
  edited the file. Log each decision below.
- [ ] Reboot when prompted (this runbook persists across the reboot)

